concept :the_grow_out do
  name "The Grow-Out"
  aka "验种", "proving a bed"
  tags :agriculture, :legibility, :locality, :water
  prominence :marginal

  prose <<~PROSE
    How a seed lot arriving under a name is proved. The lot is sown around a portion drawn from the name's own reference tin, in one bed, on one water schedule, and the bed is read against #{ref :the_seven_line_card, "the card"} rather than against itself. A lot is not asked to be uniform. It is asked to stay inside the ranges the card declared before the seed went into the ground, and to keep no more than the card's stated proportion of plants outside them.

    A bed is opened on doubt rather than on arrival. A lot with an unbroken tin, a card and a courier's mark goes straight out to the gardens. A lot that came in loose, came in under two names, or came off a bed that failed the season before is held at the bench, and none of its seed leaves until the bed closes in the autumn.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :mechanics, heading: "The Bed And The Tin"
    The reference portion goes into the four middle rows and the lot goes around it, so both take the same water on the same day from the same hand. A bed that loses its middle rows to weather or an animal is closed without a reading; the lot waits a year rather than being judged against a card it was not grown beside.

    The seven lines are read on both halves in the same hour, in the order the card sets them, and nothing in the reading uses an instrument that has to be issued: a notched stick, a split with a thumbnail, a bite at the shoulder and a bite at the tip, a crushed leaf under the nose. Two readers who disagree on a line enter both readings and the bed carries the disagreement forward.

    Passing a lot takes one season. Entering a new range on a card takes two, in two different years, because one season cannot separate what a variety is from what a year was. The second season is run by a different hand where one is available, and both sheets are filed with the weather of their year beside them.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :limits, heading: "What A Bed Cannot Settle"
    A bed proves a lot against a card. It cannot prove a card. When the same line fails in three beds in three soils, the argument moves off the lot and onto the description, and the two traditions running most of the world's proving beds answer it differently.

    The #{ref :the_garden_courts, "Garden Courts"} write a second card for the valley. A population taught for three generations by one ground and the network under it has moved, and the description that no longer fits it is the description at fault. Their beds are read by the valley's mycelium as well as by hand, through the whole season rather than at the seven readings, and none of what it reports appears on the card, whose lines were chosen so that a hand alone could judge them.

    The #{ref :the_saints_of_plenty, "Saints of Plenty"} keep the first card and enter the failure under its original number as a thing the water did. A Saints bench holds cards for varieties nobody in its settlements has brought to colour in a decade, files each season's failure against them, and sows the tin again the following spring. #{ref :the_seventh_line, "The Seventh Line"} is the bed both traditions cite.
  PROSE

  log "2026-08-09 — The bed is doubt-triggered and judged against a control planting of the reference sample, from the seed trade's grow-out test: a lot's identity is checked only when a doubt has arisen, on characters judged by eye and hand, against the reference sown alongside it. Nobody in either tradition runs a certification scheme; the mechanism was borrowed because it makes the unit of argument a single season's bed rather than a laboratory."
  log "2026-08-09 — A lot passes on declared ranges rather than on sameness, taken from the uniformity clause of variety registration, which admits variation so long as the variation was described in advance by whoever offered the variety. That decided the disagreement this entry turns on: heterogeneous material can pass a standard written that way, and what fails is a description whose ranges were written somewhere else. The two-season rule for entering a new range is the same standard's minimum of two growing cycles."
end
